A study Bible can be created by taking the complete text of one of the many
variations of the Bible translation from the internet and adding notes to
this source text. If you want to do so using \TeX{}, and to apply your
general notes to more Bible translations at the same time, then you can use the
OpBible package~[1]. This package was created via intensive collaboration of
two authors. The first author, \v Simon Kone\v cn\'y, is mainly a theologian and
in addition an average \TeX{} user. He brought the main ideas of
what to do and he was the first and main tester of the result. He started to
create his extensive note apparatus for new study Bibles in the Czech language~[2].
The second author, Petr Ol\v s\'ak,
is a \TeX{} expert. He implemented the ideas in the \TeX{} package.

\v Simon Kone\v cn\'y  wrote the first part of this article. He describes the
package from the user's point of view. Petr Ol\v s\'ak
added notes on technical background in the second section of this
article where some
interesting technical aspects are mentioned.

